let apollo11Conclusion = { Id = "44296096-c961-5921-9dd0-20297d7b51c2"; Statement = "Apollo 11 succeeded. The five 1202/1201 alarms during powered descent were Executive overflows caused by rendezvous radar coupling data unit interrupts that stole more than 10 percent of the computer's capacity; they did not degrade guidance or control. The descent continued because restart protection kept the priority guidance running, controllers applied a pre-flight decision that such intermittent alarms did not stop the descent and gave Go, Armstrong flew P66 past a boulder field (the descent had come in long because of a state-vector error at PDI) and cut off the engine with about 45 s of firing time remaining against a 20 s go/no-go point, and backup abort guidance remained available. The Mission Report lists no program alarms in ascent, which flew with the revised checklist package; this is consistent with the workaround but does not prove it. The causal roles apply to two effects: the root cause explains the alarms, the other members explain the continuation and the landing point."; ResidualUncertainty = Some "Whether the procedure or the interface specification was at fault is contested; no source supports a crew deviation. The capacity figure differs by source (over 10, about 13, up to 15 percent). No single precise fuel-remaining figure is asserted; the Mission Report's 45 s / 50 s agreement is primary, the larger slosh margin is later commentary. The alarm-free ascent is consistent with the workaround but two changes were made together and it cannot show what would have happened without them. On the surface the radar cause was a hedged working hypothesis; the mechanism was established after the flight. Pairing of individual Go calls with alarms is partly uncertain. The flown effect of the Luminary 1B software correction is not in the retrieved Apollo 11 record."; RecordedAtVersion = 430L }
